@@ -9,13 +9,26 @@ persistence is an in-memory repository behind a small interface.
   append-only `Ledger`; never stored. Only receipts, adjustments (counts,
   write-offs) and shipments write to the ledger.
 - **Allocated** — units promised to `PLACED` orders that have not shipped yet.
-- **Available** — `on_hand - allocated`, floored at zero. This is what new
-  orders may take.
+- **Held** — units in `ACTIVE` reservations (checkout holds awaiting payment).
+- **Available** — `on_hand - allocated - held`, floored at zero. This is what
+  new orders and reservations may take.
 
 Order lifecycle: `place_order` (allocates, all lines or nothing) →
 `ship_order` (writes `SHIPMENT` ledger entries) or `cancel_order` (releases
 the allocation).
 
+Reservation lifecycle: `reserve(warehouse, items, idempotency_key)` holds what
+is available per line, up to the amount asked for (lines with nothing available
+are dropped; `InsufficientStock` only if nothing at all can be held) →
+`confirm_reservation` (places a `PLACED` order for exactly the held units;
+repeat calls return the same order) or `release_reservation` (no-op if already
+released or expired). A hold lapses to `EXPIRED` at `expires_at` (creation + 20
+minutes by default, that instant included). There is no sweep job: expiry is
+applied lazily whenever reservations are read. Reusing an idempotency key with
+the same request returns the existing reservation as it is now. Reusing it with
+a different warehouse or items raises `IdempotencyConflict`. Reservations never
+touch the ledger.
+
